              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.17: Area of a parallelogram. Here, A'B'C'D' is a copy of ABCD."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.278,
            0.887,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 6.17 shows how to transform a parallelogram ABCD into a rectangle EB'C'F (at right; here, parallelogram A'B'C'D' is a copy of ABCD) with the same base b and same height h. Though the two shapes are different, their areas are the same. So, the area of the parallelogram is equal to base × height = bh."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.397,
            0.399,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.434,
            0.507,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What happens if the parallelogram is ‘thin’ (Fig. 6.18) and the foot of the perpendicular from C to AD does not lie on side AD? The construction then does not seem to work. How do we fix this ‘gap’?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.515,
            0.382,
            0.88,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.515,
                0.382,
                0.88,
                0.564
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.579,
            0.884,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here is a hint on how the problem of the ‘thin parallelogram’ can be solved."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.631,
            0.484,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.128,
                0.631,
                0.484,
                0.741
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.164,
                0.746,
                0.452,
                0.765
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.19: The thin parallelogram"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.501,
            0.635,
            0.878,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Select a point D' on DA, close to D, and a point A' on DA extended, with A'A = D'D (Fig. 6.19). Then A'BCD' is a parallelogram, and since "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta  CDD  \\cong \\Delta  BAA"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , its area is the same as that of ABCD. Now repeat this step as many times as needed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.803,
            0.399,
            0.827
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.84,
            0.867,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The area of a rectangle can be found when we know the lengths of its sides. Is the same true for a parallelogram? That is, can we find the area of a parallelogram when we know the lengths of its sides? Why or why not?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
